main.tsx: Frontend Entry Point
The query client defines shared retrieval behaviour. The application root mounts the React application;
routing and providers are defined inside App. Mutations do not retry automatically, avoiding accidental
repetition of state-changing actions.
const client = new QueryClient({
defaultOptions: {
queries: {
staleTime: 30000,
retry: 1,
refetchOnWindowFocus: true,
},
mutations: { retry: false },
},
});
ReactDOM.createRoot(document.getElementById('root')!).render(
<React.StrictMode>
<App />
</React.StrictMode>,
);
Student.tsx: Dashboard Module
The student dashboard requests upcoming registrations, memberships and recent events independently.
Typed response models describe each collection. Rendering then distinguishes loading, failed, empty and
populated states.
const regs = useQuery({
queryKey: ['registrations', 'upcoming'],
queryFn: () =>
api<Page<Registration>>('/registrations?when=upcoming'),
});
const clubs = useQuery({
queryKey: ['memberships'],
queryFn: () => api<Page<Club>>('/memberships'),
});
const events = useQuery({
queryKey: ['events', 'recent'],
queryFn: () => api<Page<CampusEvent>>('/events?sort=newest'),
});
The API helper sends same-origin credentials and obtains a CSRF token for mutations. Successful
operations invalidate relevant queries so that displayed bookings and capacity are read again from the
service. Client-side route guards improve navigation, while API authorization remains the enforcement
boundary.

